+# AutoDebug: Falcon3 Base release accuracy and acceptance failures
+
+## Scope and evidence
+
+This is an inspection-only diagnosis of the unrestricted release report
+`report_tiiuae__Falcon3-7B-Base_2026-08-12T225815+0000.md`, its small report
+JSON/runtime metadata, the eval command log, current eval configuration, and
+report acceptance code. No implementation code, credentials, dataset examples,
+or generated samples are included here.
+
+Direct observations:
+
+- IFEval completed on all 541 prompts with lm-eval's stock `ifeval` task.
+- Metrics were prompt strict 18.669%, instruction strict 30.935%, prompt loose
+  19.963%, and instruction loose 32.494%.
+- The report selected prompt-level strict and compared 18.67 to 34.3.
+- GPQA exited 1 before evaluation because `Idavidrein/gpqa` file access was
+  denied by its Hub gate; no result file was available.
+- Both eval blocks are visibly `FAIL`, but report acceptance says `PASS`,
+  labels both failures “waived,” and declares no blockers.
+- The runtime spec contains no matching known-issue waiver.
+
+## 1. IFEval: the measured key and raw-prompt shape are defensible, but the 34.3 methodology is not independently reproduced
+
+### What is confirmed
+
+The publisher's Falcon3 Base card states only that its internal pipeline used
+lm-evaluation-harness, reported raw scores, and obtained IFEval 34.3. It does
+not state the harness revision, IFEval metric column, tokenizer/BOS policy, or
+generation details.
+
+The TTI invocation uses the stock lm-eval IFEval configuration found in the
+actual eval venv:
+
+- dataset `google/IFEval`, train split, 0-shot;
+- `doc_to_text: prompt`;
+- deterministic generation, temperature 0, maximum 1280 tokens;
+- the four standard prompt/instruction strict/loose metrics.
+
+Falcon3-7B-Base is a raw pretrained model. Its card demonstrates ordinary text
+completion, and its tokenizer has no chat template. TTI's
+`apply_chat_template=False`, completions API, and raw prompt are therefore the
+correct default; substituting the Instruct model or inventing a chat wrapper
+would be methodologically wrong.
+
+`score_task_single_key()` always uses only `result_keys[0]`. The Base config's
+first key is `prompt_level_strict_acc,none`, so 18.67 is not an accidental
+average or parser error. Prompt-level strict is also the most common single
+IFEval headline metric in lm-eval leaderboard usage. The result JSON contains
+the expected key, full prompt count, and stock task version 4.0.
+
+### What is not proven
+
+The configuration incorrectly calls the publisher's 34.3 a
+`gpu_reference_score` while linking only to the publisher card. It is not an
+independently measured same-command GPU reference. Because the card does not
+identify which of the four IFEval metrics produced 34.3, exact apples-to-apples
+comparison is unproven. Notably, this run's instruction-level scores (30.94
+strict, 32.49 loose) are numerically much closer to 34.3 than prompt strict.
+That is a methodology ambiguity, not evidence that either alternative is the
+publisher's intended metric.
+
+Changing the result key merely to make the row pass is invalid. Even the best
+observed metric remains below 34.3, and the four metrics consistently show
+weak instruction following. The small result metadata plus qualitative shape
+of failures indicates severe repetitive/degenerate completion behavior, but a
+TT-vs-reference attribution cannot be made from a publisher number alone.
+
+### Verdict and decisive experiment
+
+- **Metric parser bug:** refuted. It deliberately reports prompt strict.
+- **Obvious chat-template bug:** refuted for this Base model.
+- **Reference methodology mismatch:** still possible and must be resolved.
+- **TT model correctness regression:** plausible, but not proven without an
+  identical GPU/HF run.
+
+Run the exact same eval venv, task revision, raw completions mode, tokenizer,
+seed, generation settings, and model revision against a trusted HF/GPU vLLM
+server. Record all four aggregate metrics, not samples. Then:
+
+- if GPU prompt strict is near 34.3, retain prompt strict as the score and use
+  that measured GPU result as the real reference; investigate TT model output;
+- if GPU reproduces ~18.67, the TT path is exonerated and the publisher number
+  is methodology-incompatible; identify the publisher recipe before changing
+  the configured key/reference;
+- if GPU aligns only on another metric, document that methodology explicitly
+  and change both score key and reference provenance together.
+
+An optional CPU/HF shifted-left accuracy comparison or deterministic prompt
+subset can localize a TT correctness issue, but it must use the same raw prompt
+and may not expose samples in release artifacts.
+
+## 2. GPQA: authentication is present, but the token's user lacks gated-file authorization
+
+The failure is authorization, not absence of a token and not a model/server
+failure. `Idavidrein/gpqa` is publicly discoverable but gated: unauthenticated
+or unauthorized users can view repository metadata/file names while content
+downloads remain denied. Therefore an authenticated metadata API response 200
+does not prove access to `gpqa_diamond.csv`.
+
+Hugging Face grants gated-dataset access to an individual user account after
+that user accepts the dataset conditions (or is approved). A token can only
+exercise access already granted to its owning user, and a fine-grained token
+must also include read access to that dataset. The direct `load_dataset` denial
+inside the exact eval venv shows this is not merely TTI failing to forward the
+outer environment.
+
+Safe actionable path:
+
+1. Identify the token owner without printing the token.
+2. While logged in as that same individual, visit `Idavidrein/gpqa`, accept the
+   gate/conditions, and wait for approval if required.
+3. Ensure the CI token is a read token, or a fine-grained token explicitly
+   permitted to read this dataset. Organization membership alone is not a
+   substitute for individual gated access.
+4. Validate access with a metadata/file download probe or `load_dataset` in the
+   eval venv while suppressing credential and sample output.
+5. Cache the authorized dataset in the intended HF cache and rerun GPQA with
+   the same task. Do not copy dataset content into release artifacts or bypass
+   the gate with an alternate dataset mirror.
+
+If policy prevents accepting the gate, GPQA cannot be claimed as passed. It
+must remain a blocking missing eval unless a real, issue-linked waiver is
+explicitly approved; this release's requirements disallow silently waiving it.
+
+## 3. Acceptance: EXPERIMENTAL status is being treated as an implicit eval waiver
+
+This is a source-confirmed reporting bug relative to the release contract.
+
+`report_module/acceptance_criteria.py` derives eval enforcement from
+`ModelStatusTypes.evals_enforced`. `ModelStatusTypes.EXPERIMENTAL` has no
+required benchmark tiers, and `evals_enforced` reuses that emptiness, returning
+false. In `_check_evals()`:
+
+1. each FAIL block produces `"Accuracy check failed."`;
+2. `_find_waiver()` correctly finds no known issue;
+3. because `evals_enforced` is false, lines 434-436 place the failure into the
+   category's `waived` dictionary anyway, with an “informational: model
+   status=EXPERIMENTAL” suffix;
+4. `failed` remains zero, category status becomes PASS, and top-level blockers
+   stay empty;
+5. `CategoryResult.passed` subtracts the two `waived` entries, yielding the
+   misleading `0/2 passed, 2 waived` summary.
+
+This conflates two distinct policies:
+
+- performance target tiers above a model's maturity status may be
+  informational;
+- failed accuracy/eval tasks require explicit enforcement or a declared,
+  issue-linked known-issue waiver.
+
+No waiver exists here. Model maturity is not a waiver record, and unrestricted
+release requirements explicitly make unwaived IFEval/GPQA failures blocking.
+
+### Smallest fix and tests
+
+Make eval failures enforced for every recognized model status. Keep
+status-tier relaxation only in `_check_benchmarks()`. In practical terms,
+`_evals_enforced()` should not return false for EXPERIMENTAL (or
+`ModelStatusTypes.evals_enforced` should always be true if no other caller
+needs the old policy). Continue to demote an eval only when `_find_waiver()`
+matches a real `known_issues` entry.
+
+Focused tests:
+
+1. Parameterize EXPERIMENTAL/FUNCTIONAL/COMPLETE/TOP_PERF with an eval FAIL and
+   no known issues; every case must produce category FAIL, a blocker, zero
+   waived entries, and top-level acceptance FAIL.
+2. Add a matching EVALS known issue; verify exactly that task is waived and the
+   serialized reason is preserved.
+3. Use two failures (one accuracy failure, one explicit task/rc failure) and no
+   waiver; assert summary says `0/2 passed, 2 failed`, never `2 waived`.
+4. Verify EXPERIMENTAL benchmark tiers remain informational so this fix does
+   not change performance-maturity policy.
+5. Regenerate acceptance from the existing small report schema and assert both
+   IFEval and GPQA appear as blockers.
+
+## Final status
+
+- The existing report's top-level PASS is invalid and must not be used as a
+  release handoff.
+- GPQA is blocked on user-level gated-dataset authorization.
+- IFEval is a real failed row under the configured prompt-strict methodology;
+  whether it is a TT regression or publisher-method mismatch requires the
+  identical HF/GPU control above.
+- No accuracy failure may be removed, re-keyed, prompt-aligned, context-capped,
+  or relabeled merely to make release acceptance pass.
